Title: "Relations"
Id: "61ac6940-a021-4d54-bc78-910c75ce3a04"


Text: "A relation records how the elements of a set stand to one another.
       Formally it is nothing more than a set of ordered pairs -- a subset of
       the Cartesian square -- but this simple idea is remarkably powerful. This
       chapter treats relations in general and then singles out the *equivalence
       relations*, whose classes partition a set and let one pass to a quotient.
       The construction of the integers in the next chapter rests entirely on
       this machinery."
Id: "c29d6027-fe93-4e6d-94df-f72a802fedcb"


SectionTitle: "Binary Relations and Their Properties"
Id: "c43de393-3ca6-474f-9c32-2996cb1b320b"


[\relation:?on{X}]
Declares: R \:subset:/ (.X \.set.cross./ X.)
when: X is \set
Enables:
. capability: R(x_, y_) :=> (x_, y_) "in"? R
  written: "x_? \: R \: y_?"
Documented:
. called: "relation on $X?$"
. description: "A relation on a set $X$ is a subset $R$ of $X \times X$. One
                writes $x \mathrel{R} y$ to mean that the pair $(x, y)$ belongs
                to $R$, so a relation is just a precise record of which ordered
                pairs are related."
Id: "5ace60a2-cc66-4cea-818a-538f877b5f97"


Text: "A relation is reflexive if every element is related to itself."
Id: "35a87f95-8d23-44a0-ae4f-70d4384a3db6"


[\(reflexive)::relation:?on{X}]
Refines: R
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: x "in" X
  then: R(x, x)
Documented:
. adjective: "reflexive"
. description: "A relation $R$ on $X$ is reflexive if $x \mathrel{R} x$ for all
                $x \in X$."
Id: "8db6967b-79de-4fd6-8769-fcd651c7c6ee"


Text: "A relation is symmetric if the order of elements in a related pair can be
       reversed."
Id: "b2c8da90-8a30-4542-a3a3-f81bf989953a"


[\(symmetric)::relation:?on{X}]
Refines: R
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: x, y "in" X
  where: R(x, y)
  then: R(y, x)
Documented:
. adjective: "symmetric"
. description: "A relation $R$ on $X$ is symmetric if $x \mathrel{R} y$ implies
                $y \mathrel{R} x$."
Id: "273288f5-a313-4140-a28c-16ac7e8ba72b"


Text: "A relation is transitive if relationships chain through intermediate
       elements."
Id: "5965ba24-25b8-4a16-92fc-035988cfb21c"


[\(transitive)::relation:?on{X}]
Refines: R
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: x, y, z "in" X
  where:
  . R(x, y)
  . R(y, z)
  then: R(x, z)
Documented:
. adjective: "transitive"
. description: "A relation $R$ on $X$ is transitive if $x \mathrel{R} y$ and
                $y \mathrel{R} z$ imply $x \mathrel{R} z$."
Id: "8baba6bf-25f4-48b4-84f9-4b2db59ccf47"


Text: "A relation is antisymmetric if two elements can only be mutually related
       when they are identical."
Id: "bdbeccce-dfdb-4d54-8b0c-92cae82c56d7"


[\(antisymmetric)::relation:?on{X}]
Refines: R
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: x, y "in" X
  where:
  . R(x, y)
  . R(y, x)
  then: x = y
Documented:
. adjective: "antisymmetric"
. description: "A relation $R$ on $X$ is antisymmetric if $x \mathrel{R} y$ and
                $y \mathrel{R} x$ imply $x = y$."
Id: "8ed4e2b2-f1b7-4131-8ae2-2834e71805fa"


Text: "The inverse relation swaps the roles of the two arguments in every pair."
Id: "ece9bae3-c088-459e-9cd1-7a650d9b3681"


[\relation.inverse:of{R}]
Defines: S \:subset:/ (.X \.set.cross./ X.)
using: X is \set
when: R is \relation:on{X}
expresses:
. let: x, y "in" X
  then:
  . have: S(x, y)
    iff: R(y, x)
Documented:
. called: "inverse relation of $R?$"
. written: "R+?^{-1}"
. description: "The inverse relation $R^{-1}$ consists of all pairs $(x, y)$
                such that $(y, x) \in R$."
Id: "b182f12e-f94f-40e7-b5b7-c84dd243c262"


SectionTitle: "Equivalence Relations and Quotients"
Id: "66f53348-5a61-4763-9645-64390784903b"


Text: "The most important relations are those that behave like a generalized
       equality. An *equivalence relation* is reflexive, symmetric, and
       transitive: every element is related to itself, the relation is symmetric
       between pairs, and it chains through intermediate elements. These three
       laws are exactly what is needed to regard related elements as
       interchangeable."
Id: "b906ce0b-34d4-4b24-b168-d6a8d05efc92"


[\equivalence.relation:on{X}]
Declares: R is \relation:on{X}
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. [reflexive]
  forAll: x "in" X
  then: R(x, x)
. [symmetric]
  forAll: x, y "in" X
  where: R(x, y)
  then: R(y, x)
. [transitive]
  forAll: x, y, z "in" X
  where:
  . R(x, y)
  . R(y, z)
  then: R(x, z)
Documented:
. called: "equivalence relation on $X?$"
. description: "An equivalence relation on $X$ is a relation that is reflexive
                ($x \mathrel{R} x$), symmetric (if $x \mathrel{R} y$ then $y
                \mathrel{R} x$), and transitive (if $x \mathrel{R} y$ and $y
                \mathrel{R} z$ then $x \mathrel{R} z$). Such a relation sorts the
                elements of $X$ into classes of mutually related elements."
Id: "2394a97b-5960-4919-98db-b95ec3870343"


Text: "Each element gives rise to its *equivalence class*, the set of all
       elements related to it. Two classes are either identical or disjoint, so
       the classes carve $X$ into non-overlapping pieces."
Id: "308e4d46-7205-4977-9583-486771d4ca48"


[\equivalence.class:of{x}:over{R}]
Defines: C := \set@{y_ : y_ "in" X | R(x, y_)}
using: X is \set
when:
. x "in" X
. R is \equivalence.relation:on{X}
Documented:
. called: "equivalence class of $x?$ on $R?$"
. written: "\left [ x? \right ]_{R?}"
. description: "The equivalence class $[x]_R$ of an element $x$ is the set of
                all elements related to $x$ by $R$. Every element lies in
                exactly one class, and two elements share a class precisely when
                they are related."
Id: "2fae575c-13c9-498b-b14a-e471f0e3cfdc"


Text: "Gathering the equivalence classes into a single set forms the *quotient*
       of $X$ by $R$. Passing to the quotient is the standard way of turning a
       relation of sameness into genuine equality, and it is the tool used next
       to build the integers from the naturals."
Id: "7435ef90-380d-4429-a369-37d652479b85"


[X \.mod./ R]
Defines: I := \set@{ \equivalence.class:of{x_}:over{R} : x_ "in" X }
when:
. X is \set
. R is \equivalence.relation:on{X}
Documented:
. called: "$X?$ mod $R?$"
. written: "X? / R?"
. description: "The quotient set $X / R$ is the set whose elements are the
                equivalence classes of $R$. It collapses each class to a single
                point, so that elements related by $R$ become equal in the
                quotient."
Id: "cea0d898-a18d-44dc-b04c-ffb2f0cde26e"


SectionTitle: "Order Relations"
Id: "88c4093d-566b-48bb-baea-9926345a182f"


Text: "While equivalence relations model symmetry and indistinguishability,
       *order relations* model hierarchy, comparison, and precedence."
Id: "b69a5d44-be1d-4f87-bab6-f5c4eccf221c"


[\partial.order:on{X}]
Declares: R is \relation:on{X}
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. [reflexive]
  forAll: x "in" X
  then: R(x, x)
. [antisymmetric]
  forAll: x, y "in" X
  where:
  . R(x, y)
  . R(y, x)
  then: x = y
. [transitive]
  forAll: x, y, z "in" X
  where:
  . R(x, y)
  . R(y, z)
  then: R(x, z)
Documented:
. called: "partial order on $X?$"
. description: "A partial order on $X$ is a reflexive, antisymmetric, and
                transitive relation."
Id: "656a5a0a-77e8-42ea-857c-4f7d8eda00ac"


[\total.order:on{X}]
Declares: R is \partial.order:on{X}
when: X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: x, y "in" X
  then:
  . anyOf:
    . R(x, y)
    . R(y, x)
Documented:
. called: "total order on $X?$"
. description: "A total (or linear) order on $X$ is a partial order in which
                every two elements are comparable."
Id: "40d12e9b-449e-49b9-a292-9388147d3e69"
